{"name":"Sail On","hex":"#4575ad","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of fabric attached to a boat and arranged such that it causes the wind to drive the boat along. The sail may be attached to the boat via a combination of mast, spars and ropes.","The concept of a sail or sails, as if a substance."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To be impelled or driven forward by the action of wind upon sails, as a ship on water; to be impelled on a body of water by steam or other power.","To move through or on the water; to swim, as a fish or a waterfowl."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sail On (Imperials album)","blocks":[{"text":"Sail On is the 24th studio album by the Christian music vocal group the Imperials. Released in 1977, it is their first album on Word Records' DaySpring label. It is also the first appearance of two new members, Russ Taff on lead vocals and David Will on baritone vocals, in addition to founding member, bass vocalist Armond Morales and tenor Jim Murray. Taff and Will replaced Sherman Andrus and Terry Blackwood, who went on to form the Christian music duo Andrus, Blackwood and Company in 1977 after the release of the 1976 Imperials' album Just Because."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8a/Imperials_Sail_On.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sail_On_(Imperials_album)"},{"title":"The Best of Muddy Waters","blocks":[{"text":"The Best of Muddy Waters is a greatest hits album by Muddy Waters released by Chess Records in April 1958. The twelve songs were originally issued as singles between 1948 and 1954 and most appeared in Billboard's top 10 Rhythm & Blues Records charts."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/51/Best_of_Muddy_Waters_1958_Chess_Records.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Best_of_Muddy_Waters"},{"title":"Sail On (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Sail On\" is a Commodores song written by Lionel Richie from their 1979 album Midnight Magic. Released as the first of three singles from the album, it was produced by both Commodores and James Anthony Carmichael. The song reached the top ten on both the US and UK music charts that same year. \"Sail On\" got as high as #4 on the Billboard Hot 100 chart while it made it all the way to #1 on the Cash Box Top 100 singles chart. Richie later recorded the song with Tim McGraw for 2012's Tuskegee."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sail_On_(song)"},{"title":"Sail","blocks":[{"text":"A sail is a tensile structure, which is made from fabric or other membrane materials, that uses wind power to propel sailing craft, including sailing ships, sailboats, windsurfers, ice boats, and even sail-powered land vehicles. Sails may be made from a combination of woven materials—including canvas or polyester cloth, laminated membranes or bonded filaments, usually in a three- or four-sided shape."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Chesapeake.jpg/330px-Chesapeake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sail"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Sailing_Vessel_Odyssey.jpg/500px-Sailing_Vessel_Odyssey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Sailing_Vessel_Odyssey.jpg/500px-Sailing_Vessel_Odyssey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASailing_Vessel_Odyssey.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/L-2013-Oracle-Team-USA-17.jpg/250px-L-2013-Oracle-Team-USA-17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/63/L-2013-Oracle-Team-USA-17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/L-2013-Oracle-Team-USA-17.jpg/250px-L-2013-Oracle-Team-USA-17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AL-2013-Oracle-Team-USA-17.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Maler_der_Grabkammer_des_Menna_013.jpg/500px-Maler_der_Grabkammer_des_Menna_013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Maler_der_Grabkammer_des_Menna_013.jpg/1280px-Maler_der_Grabkammer_des_Menna_013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Maler_der_Grabkammer_des_Menna_013.jpg/500px-Maler_der_Grabkammer_des_Menna_013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Egyptian sailing ship, c. 1422–1411 BCE","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaler_der_Grabkammer_des_Menna_013.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Bgl3t.jpg/500px-Bgl3t.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Bgl3t.jpg/500px-Bgl3t.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A traditional Maldivian Baghlah with a fore-and-aft lateen rig","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABgl3t.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Atlas_pittoresque_pl_096.jpg/500px-Atlas_pittoresque_pl_096.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Atlas_pittoresque_pl_096.jpg/1280px-Atlas_pittoresque_pl_096.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Atlas_pittoresque_pl_096.jpg/500px-Atlas_pittoresque_pl_096.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fijian voyaging outrigger boat with a crab claw sail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAtlas_pittoresque_pl_096.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Iranun_Lanong_warship_by_Rafael_Monle%C3%B3n_%281890%29.jpg/500px-Iranun_Lanong_warship_by_Rafael_Monle%C3%B3n_%281890%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Iranun_Lanong_warship_by_Rafael_Monle%C3%B3n_%281890%29.jpg/500px-Iranun_Lanong_warship_by_Rafael_Monle%C3%B3n_%281890%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Philippine lanong with tanja sails","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIranun_Lanong_warship_by_Rafael_Monle%C3%B3n_(1890).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Points_of_sail--close-hauled_%28right%29_and_down_wind_%28left%29.jpg/500px-Points_of_sail--close-hauled_%28right%29_and_down_wind_%28left%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Points_of_sail--close-hauled_%28right%29_and_down_wind_%28left%29.jpg/500px-Points_of_sail--close-hauled_%28right%29_and_down_wind_%28left%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aerodynamic forces for two points of sail. Left-hand boat:Down wind—predominant drag propels the boat with little heeling moment. Right-hand boat:Up wind (close-hauled)—predominant lift both propels the boat and contributes to heel.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APoints_of_sail--close-hauled_(right)_and_down_wind_(left).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Sail_angles_of_attack_and_resulting_flow_patterns.jpg/500px-Sail_angles_of_attack_and_resulting_flow_patterns.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Sail_angles_of_attack_and_resulting_flow_patterns.jpg/500px-Sail_angles_of_attack_and_resulting_flow_patterns.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sail angles of attack and resulting (idealized) flow patterns that provide propulsive lift.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASail_angles_of_attack_and_resulting_flow_patterns.jpg"}],"sounds":{"sail":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/46/En-us-sail.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/4/46/En-us-sail.ogg/En-us-sail.ogg.mp3"}]}}}